US Supreme Court continues to remove Donald Trump barriers
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States Supreme Court has continued to remove new barriers, ruling that he has the immunity to avoid any prosecution involving his actions during his presidency. This is the second obstacle to overcome and after the first obstacle is the decision on March 4, 2024 to grant him the right to be a presidential candidate.
President Joe Biden has condemned the Supreme Court's decision, which ruled that the president has all the privileges of avoiding prosecution for formal core actions. The decision by all US presidents to avoid prosecution over their official actions during his presidency seems to be a signal that paves the way for Donald Trump, who is on his way back. Re-emerges in power at the White House.
The nine-member Supreme Court, with six judges in favor and three against, was marred by minority dissent, even though incumbent President Joe Biden saw it as a wrong and excessive decision. The limits of the power of the US president.
"What the US president has done is not always to do what he wants to do by going beyond its borders," Biden told Americans. "The decision by the Supreme Court was an unprecedented danger because of the ambitions of the ruling power and that no law can stop it," Biden said. "Even the United States Supreme Court."
Following a Supreme Court ruling in March, Donald Trump became the Republican presidential candidate after a landslide victory over Nikki Haley. The Supreme Court's second ruling in July on Donald Trump's progress comes amid criticism from his Democratic campaign.
The 81-year-old President of the United States is trying to overcome some of the concerns that former supporters have raised about his inability to negotiate a victory over Donald Trump. Seen in a 90-minute discussion last weekend that shows he failed, and the big blame was due to his old age, which did not allow Joe Biden to do those things well.
