These countries have complained that Russia's satellite interference has caused serious consequences, such as disruption of GPS signals, endanger air traffic control and disruption of children's television programs, depicting the violent images of the war in Ukraine. Original. The ITU has expressed grave concern over Russia's actions and urged Russia to stop them immediately.
The ITU said the disruption to the French and Swedish satellite networks appeared to have originated from Earth stations located in the Moscow, Kaliningrad and Pavlovsk regions. These actions are considered by the ITU to be alarming and unacceptable.
The ITU has called for a meeting between the affected countries and Russia to resolve the issue and prevent a recurrence. However, Russia has not yet responded to the call. Russia has denied allegations of satellite interference and has even complained about satellite interference by NATO countries.
The ITU is a United Nations agency responsible for managing and coordinating the global satellite system. It has 193 member countries and is based in Geneva. The ITU's constitution mandates it to coordinate efforts to eliminate harmful interference.
