The minister said that in the past, the site was a small airfield where Japanese powers invaded Cambodia and used it as an airport to support air warfare. When the war ended, the site was used to transport agricultural goods from Pursat to Phnom Penh during the Lon Nol period, when most of the national roads occupied by the Khmer Rouge were inaccessible.
After the liberation on January 7, the site was discontinued and some people enjoyed it. Mr. Keo Rattanak said that in 2018, under his own initiative, this location was designated as state property for the development and construction of a stadium and was strongly supported by Samdech Techo when he met with garment factory workers on March 20, 2019.
He added that the work we need to do is not only the stadium, but also supermarkets, restaurants, cafes, electric charging stations ... to attract investors who have invested in Cambodia, in general and in particular in the region. Krakor Special Economy can have a decent place to eat, hang out and exercise.
