HANOI: Vietnam's economic growth accelerated in the second quarter due to strong exports, but rising inflation remains a challenge for the Southeast Asian nation, government data showed on Saturday. According to Reuters.
Gross domestic product (GDP) is projected to grow 6.93 percent in the second quarter from a year earlier, Reuters reported, citing data released by the General Statistics Office of the Vietnamese Government (GSO). Last year, it was higher than the 5.87 percent growth in the first quarter.
Vietnam, a major exporter of smartphones, electronics and clothing, is trying to boost business activity after failing to meet last year's growth target due to weak global demand and energy shortages.
"Vietnam's socio-economic situation continues to be positive, with better quarters each year than last year," the GSO said in a statement.
Vietnam's exports in the first half of this year rose 14.5 percent year on year to 190 billion USD, while industrial production rose 10.9 percent year on year. Last year. According to the GSO office.
Vietnam's inflationary pressures are rising, with consumer prices in June rising 4.32% year-on-year, close to the government's annual inflation target of 4.5% for the year. This. According to Reuters.
Thai government to hold talks with Malaysia to boost bilateral trade
The Thai government will meet with its Malaysian counterpart at a Joint Trade Committee (JTC) meeting on Thursday aimed at boosting bilateral trade to $ 30 billion by 2025. According to the Bangkok Post.
Chotima said the meeting would be Thailand's first JTC meeting with ASEAN this year. She added that it would boost trade between the two countries from $ 25 billion by 2023 to $ 30 billion next year and accelerate Malaysia's move to open up more markets for Thai agricultural products.
In addition, the two countries will seek to establish new partnerships that will benefit both economies and entrepreneurs under Phumtham's policy of promoting trade, reducing trade barriers and boosting trade. Bilateral is favorable. The meeting will also be an opportunity to boost economic connectivity along the border.
According to the Bangkok Post, Malaysia is Thailand's first trading partner in Asean and ranks fourth in the overall rankings. Trade between the two countries totaled $ 25 billion last year and $ 10.787 billion during the first five months of this year.
