Blind and HIV-positive women receive a motorbike donation from Mr. Chea Somethy and Ms.
(Phnom Penh): On the afternoon of June 27, 2024, the family of a blind and HIV-positive woman, Ms. John Lumong, received a motorbike from Mr. Chea Somethy, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation and Ms. Chea Phalkun.
Seeing her plight on May 08, 2024, Mr. Chap To, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, informed Minister Chea Somethy about the situation and hardships in the daily life of the above family. .
After receiving this information, under the kindness and compassion of the Minister, Minister Chea Somethy donated a 2008 Honda Dream series motorcycle worth $ 650 to the family of Ms. John Lum Ong to facilitate their daily livelihood.
Mrs. John Lumong and her family are very grateful and excited to receive the support of her family from Mr. Chea Somethy and Ms. Chea Phalkun.
Please note that in the past, Ms. Nhem Morakot and Mr. Chap To, as well as the team of the Samneang Music Association for the Disabled, always take care to support food and some money with various philanthropists to her family as well.
Japan to risk prolonging interest rates in US, Europe (Video inside) (Tokyo): The Japanese government said on Thursday that the Japanese economy is slowly recovering. But warned in its monthly report that high interest rates in the United States and Europe pose risks that hurt its economy. According to Reuters.
These risks to Japan's economy include increased pressure on a weaker yen, a Japanese cabinet official who compiled a monthly report for June said. The official asked not to be named because he did not have the right to speak to reporters about the issue.
The Japanese yen weakened more than 160 yen on Thursday, hitting a low of 160.88 against the dollar, raising concerns about gains, Reuters reported. Higher living costs. The sharp fall in the yen has raised concerns about the cost of living through rising imports of goods, affecting resource-poor Japan.
The yen depreciated about 2 percent in June and is down 12 percent this year due to sharp differences between US and Japanese interest rates.
"Japan's economy is recovering moderately, although recently it seems to be stagnant," the monthly report said. "The economy may face downside risks as a result of the continued rise in interest rates in the United States and Europe," the report added. "There will be full focus on volatility in the financial and capital markets." According to Reuters.
