Preah Ang Duong Hospital trains parents with deaf children about the importance of rehabilitation after deaf surgery
(Phnom Penh): Preah Ang Duong Hospital this morning, June 27, 2024, opened a presentation program on parenting education on the importance of rehabilitation after deaf surgery.
The event was presided over by Professor Lou Ly Kheang, Director of Ang Duong Hospital, with the participation of experts from Australia and the directors of hearLIFE Care Centers in Cambodia, Thailand, Myanmar and Laos.
Professor Lou Ly Kheang said that the opening of this educational program aims to train parents about the importance of rehabilitation after deaf surgery. Rehabilitation is very important for children who have undergone deaf surgery so that they can start living a normal, normal life.
"We believe that parents have a very important role to play in providing support, education and care for their children," he said. Parents are the ones who help children have the energy to participate in rehabilitation and increase their happiness. "Parents, please take care of us and we will continue to move forward together to ensure that our children have a good future after deaf surgery."
The parents of the deaf child expressed their satisfaction with the fun they have been able to contribute to helping their child regain hearing after surgery.
A mother whose daughter has just undergone deaf surgery in Koh Kong province has thanked Preah Ang Duong Hospital for treating her daughter to hear and speak like normal people. Listen 80%.
In particular, the mother also called on people with small children to monitor their activities, if you do not ignore the call or show that you do not hear the voice, please take the child to the hospital immediately. Time.
"I took my daughter to two hospitals in the past to be treated for deafness, but she still could not be cured, so she decided to take her to the hospital," she said. Ang Duong underwent surgery and after the operation, the doctors taught me how to care for my baby under surgery. As a result, my baby was able to hear and speak like normal children.
According to the director of Preah Ang Duong Hospital, a total of 47 patients underwent surgery in Cambodia at Preah Ang Duong Hospital, corresponding to 61 ears, 17 men and 30 women, who started the first case surgery on the 16th. October 2018.
