Experts Condemn Central's Unprofessional Report Affecting Garment Sector
(Phnom Penh): Reporting experts have strongly criticized Central's unprofessional reporting, which is selfish, serving foreign ambitions by inflating facts, leading to serious problems for workers' work. Approximately 1 million people in the garment sector in Cambodia.
Dr. Hong Vann: An economist at the Royal Academy of Cambodia states that the principles of research and reporting must adhere to the ethics of voluntary and non-coercive answering. At the same time, he says, there must be certainty about the findings as a result of that research.
However, Dr. Hong Vann noted that the foreign-funded Central Organization violated the professional ethics of the research, creating findings that distorted the privacy of unions and workers.
"This distortion is seriously affecting the overall development process of the country as a whole, especially the garment, footwear and all travel equipment factories, which will create about 1 million jobs for Cambodians and bring about an income to the nation," he said. "17 billion a year is a serious impact."
The expert explained that if there were any violations and restrictions on workers' rights in Cambodia, the local unions representing the workers as well as the workers themselves would receive legal knowledge and welfare through various organizations that visited The training will protest on its own, without the need for a central organization to issue a report.
Similarly, Dr. Chhit Bunthong, a philosopher and researcher at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, agrees with Dr. Hong Vann: that the unprofessional nature of the Central organization that issued the report did not reflect the facts and that the report did not receive complete data. No truth creates misunderstandings that cause riots by workers or those who are sick of workers in society.
He says Central's inflated report also affects investors and buyers who no longer want to invest in Cambodia. Not only that, he added that other countries in the European Union will put pressure on Cambodia because of misunderstandings about the implementation of workers' rights.
Dr. Chhot Bunthong emphasized: "A report affects the whole society, so it requires the disclosure of the truth, which is the truth, not just negligently, because when it goes out, it affects the national interest, affects the interests of the boss. Affect the interests and dignity of national institutions related to the labor sector.
In a statement issued on the night of June 26, Central acknowledged that the sample size of its research was limited and did not represent the actual experience of Cambodian workers. According to its report released on June 4, the freedom of unions in Cambodia as a whole is being undermined.
