US welcomes Vietnamese PM for economic talks after Putin's visit to Hanoi
The United States has welcomed the Vietnamese Minister of Planning and Investment for talks on strengthening economic cooperation and said the two countries' ties are stronger than ever. This is according to the release by Reuters on Wednesday, June 26, 2024.
The reception comes just days after Hanoi disappointed Washington with the presence of Russian President Vladimir Putin in Vietnam. According to the State Department, this week's discussion will cover economic security, semiconductors, climate investment, digital economy, information technology, energy and mining.
Last year, Washington upgraded US-Vietnam relations to a "comprehensive strategic partnership." According to Fernandez, US-Vietnam trade is now worth $ 124 billion, making Vietnam the ninth largest US trading partner.
The United States will decide whether to increase Vietnam's status in its market economy by July 26, which will reduce Vietnam's import tariffs, as Vietnam is currently a non-US market economy.
