(Bangkok): A regulation that allows the Revenue Department of the Thai Ministry of Economy to levy a 7% value-added tax on imported products worth less than 1,500 baht has been published in the Thai Government Gazette. Effective July 5. According to the Bangkok Post.
The Thai cabinet has approved the rule in response to complaints from small businesses about the difficulty of competing with cheap foreign goods, especially products sold online. Imported goods sold for less than 1,500 baht are currently exempt from VAT in Thailand.
The Bangkok Post reported, citing a Ministry of Finance official, that the provision would take effect until December 31, giving authorities time to draft amendments to the law to make it permanent.
Thai Prime Minister Setha Thavisin also noted the trend of misrepresentations made on cheap Chinese products in the FTA to avoid paying value-added tax, which he said should be waived. According to the Bangkok Post.
Cambodian National Labor Union, representing more than 70,000 workers, warns of large-scale strike against Central Organization
(Phnom Penh): The National Labor Union of Cambodia, representing 70,000 garment workers, has announced that it will launch a large-scale strike against the activities of the Center for Labor and Human Rights (Central) and will submit a statement to the Ministry of Interior. Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, embassy buyers and some other activities.
The announcement of the large-scale strike took place on the morning of June 24, 2024, during a press conference at the office of the National Labor Union of Cambodia in Sangkat Choam Chao 2, Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh, on the issuance of a statement to disbelievers. On the "Central" report, which disseminates distorting the facts, interfering, inciting, intimidating, dividing, and painting unions in Cambodia.
Sam Soeun, president of the National Labor Union of Cambodia, said the Central report had undermined efforts to build the labor sector, as well as respect for the rights and practice of freedom of association in Cambodia. The full text of Central's 12-page report, released on June 4, 2024, does not reflect the actual disability of the working conditions in Cambodia, of which more than 700 factories interviewed only 10 unions of 14 factories.
"Thus, the term Central is being used in conjunction with independent unions and yellow unions has seriously affected the exercise of the rights of all unions that actively work to serve and protect the rights and interests of workers," he said. "
The President of the National Labor Union of Cambodia also stated that Central has no real professional in assessing the working situation in Cambodia. This organization is just an NGO registered with the Ministry of Interior, which under the Law on Trade Unions, Central has no right to interfere or fulfill its mission in the field of labor in Cambodia.
He said Central had overreacted, often conspiring to form unions in factories to attack existing unions in factories, accusing them of not providing independent protection. To real workers.
The National Labor Union of Cambodia has requested the relevant authorities to investigate and take legal action against those behind Central and to investigate the assistance that Central has received to carry out regular activities with the intention of inflating facts, inciting and destroying. Security of public order in order to cause instability and harm to workers and society as a whole.
