The first group of 5,000 people who have been called up by the military for military service will begin their duties by the end of June, military sources said on Monday. As Myanmar's military government continues to struggle to quell the insurgency against its coup. This was released by the AFP news agency on Monday, June 24, 2024.
Myanmar's military regime decided to re-enact a law requiring people to serve in the military in February this year, three years after it staged a coup d'état and sparked an armed uprising that has spread across the country. The above law allows the military regime to summon men between the ages of 18-35 and women between the ages of 18-27 to serve in the army for at least two years.
According to sources from the Myanmar military, AFP has received the first group of those who began military training in early April and will end their training later this month. Upon completion of the training, a total of 5,000 new recruits will be deployed at various military command centers around the country to begin their duties.
