More than 40,000 workers' representatives petition donors for reconsideration of support for Central
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Trade Union Confederation of Ponleu Workers, representing 40,000 workers, filed a petition at the US Embassy this morning, June 24, requesting the USAID Country Director to reconsider funding for the Labor Alliance Center. And human rights abbreviated as "Central".
Sea Kunthea, secretary general of the Cambodian Trade Union Confederation of Light Workers and President of the Cambodian Women's Trade Union Federation, said the main purpose of the petition was to get USAID donors to reconsider their funding package for Central and to guide the organization. This acts in a transparent manner and in accordance with its own statutes, especially in accordance with Cambodian law.
She stated that the Central Report, released on June 4, 2024, did not reflect the real situation of workers in Cambodia, as it mentioned restrictions, intimidation, threats, harassment and closure of workers' rights in Cambodia. By interviewing only 14 of the 703 factories of the Better Factories Cambodia program of the International Labor Organization (ILO-BFC).
She stated that the content of the report was baseless, affecting national honor, affecting job stability and the common interests of workers in Cambodia as a whole. In particular, this report is politically motivated and incites discrimination against unions and disrupts trade unions in Cambodia.
In addition, Central is an organization registered with the Ministry of Interior, but steals funds to form unions in some factories, which is contrary to its statutes. The activities that Central has done and is continuing to do are not in accordance with the statutes of the Law on Trade Unions, the Law on Associations and the Cambodian Labor Law.
"Central is an organization that issues politically motivated reports, incites unions and unions and misleads each other, affects the stability of unions in Cambodia, affects jobs and workers' interests," she said. The whole of Cambodia. ”
Workers' Representative Secretary-General of the Cambodian Trade Union Confederation, Ponleu Workers, expressed deep concern over the impact on job security and the interests of workers and urged the USAID Director to reconsider the budget support for activities. Central Organization and ask Central Organization to act transparently and in accordance with its statutes.
