However, a spokesman for the Ministry of Agriculture urged farmers in all provinces to continue to increase vigilance, monitor their crops regularly and take timely action to avoid losses. Serious about their crops. Citizens who donate ceilings to museums in Siem Reap want to participate in promoting the role of ceilings in Cambodian society.
(Siem Reap): People who donate ceilings to the Museum of Asian Traditional Textiles in Siem Reap are willing to participate in promoting the role of ceilings in Cambodian society to the public, as well as to cultivate a loving and caring attitude. Another face.
It should be noted that the Museum of Asian Traditional Textiles, which is managed by the APSARA Authority, received three ceiling handouts from a silk weaver in Prey Kabas district, Takeo province in mid-June 2024. This ceiling has a Vesant Cheatak story lantern, which is full of art and skill.
Lim Meach, 91, a resident of Trapeang Stok village, Taing Yap commune, Prey Kabas district, said she has been weaving since the age of 20 and can also weave ceilings. Although he is too old to weave today, he donated one of the last ceilings to the Museum of Asian Traditional Textiles for preservation and display to national and international tourists.
Mrs. Lim Meach was very happy to donate the ceiling to the museum, as she wanted the next generation of Cambodian children to know and take care not to lose it.
Ms. Kong Sopheak, Textile Conservation Officer of the Museum of Asian Traditional Textiles, said that the museum has recently received 44 collections from 27 families in Takeo province. Among them, there are three hollow ceilings, and these hollow ceilings play an important role, such as the folded statues, which they weave to be placed in shrines or pagodas, and have subscribers to give to monks.
Ms. Sopheak continued that the artwork of weaving the ceiling of the people of Takeo province is many generations, which transfer this skill to this day. Ms. Kong Sopheak explained that at present, the next generation of Cambodians do not know much about the ceiling, only the holes that are woven for cutting clothes.
