Senior health officials urge all doctors to take good care of pregnant women before childbirth to reduce postpartum risks
(Phnom Penh): Prof. M. Sithikar, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, requested the leaders of hospitals and health centers across the country to pay special attention to pregnant women, especially to establish relationships with them when receiving antenatal services. First, to ask about health details and advise on how to take care of yourself and your baby to be healthy, especially to encourage regular check-ups.
This statement was made by the professor on the occasion of the opening of the annual review workshop on emergency services for obstetrics and neonatal care this weekend at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
Prof. M. Sithikar said that prevention is definitely better than cure, so I would like to urge all leaders, especially in the provinces, to take care of pregnant women before giving birth.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Health added that, despite taking care of pregnant women before giving birth, all doctors should not be negligent, must continue to care until after childbirth, because the risk may occur forever. .
Therefore, this workshop is very important to find out the challenges and factors that cause women to die, especially postpartum hemorrhage, and the plan to improve the emergency services for maternity and newborn care for 2024-2025. To accelerate the reduction of maternal and infant mortality, especially maternal mortality.
As a result, after the introduction of emergency maternity and neonatal care services, maternal and infant mortality rates have dropped significantly: * Infant mortality has dropped from 27/1000 live births in 2010. By 8/1000, born in 2022.
This was confirmed by Dr. Kim Rathna, director of the Maternal and Child Health Center. Spokesman of the General Commission: 2 or 126 more vehicles were inspected and fined on 23 June 2024
