WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States on Friday sanctioned the top 12 leaders of Russia-based cybersecurity firm Kaspersky Lab. The announcement comes a day after a ban on the sale of the Russian company's popular anti-virus software on national security grounds.
The broad sanctions target most of Kaspersky Lab's senior executives, including the chief operating officer, while the CEO and the company itself are excluded, the AFP news agency reported, citing a statement issued by the US Treasury Department. .
"The United States will take the necessary steps to hold those responsible for mediation responsible, otherwise these actions will be triggered," he added.
He added that this poses an "unacceptable risk to national or US security, the safety and security of the American people."
The announcement comes after a lengthy investigation by the Commerce Ministry found that "Kaspersky's ongoing operations in the United States pose a national security risk due to the Russian government's cyber-attack capabilities." "Or lead Kaspersky operations."
In a statement sent to AFP, Kaspersky vowed to "seek all possible legal options to maintain its current operations and communications." The company added that the company "has not been involved in any activities that threaten US national security."
