"This project will help Cambodia achieve sustainable water security and achieve greater agricultural productivity," said Maria Salim, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia.
She added: "The current investment in climate resilience, better planning and infrastructure, can not only immediately respond to the water needs of Cambodian farmers and households, but also "It also serves as a foundation for long-term water services."
Although Cambodia has abundant water resources, differences in seasonal and regional rainfall in the country pose many challenges to urban and rural water supply. The weather forecast says floods and droughts will become more frequent and worsening, putting more pressure on the country's ability to manage its freshwater resources. This will affect food production and economic growth.
The project will be implemented over a five-year period by the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
The project will strengthen water resources management by expanding hydrological stations, updating policies and regulations, developing river basin management plans with weather information, and strengthening the functions of national and provincial water authorities.
Household water supply and irrigation systems will be rehabilitated and improved, and water user farmers' communities will receive training and technical assistance through projects for the operation and maintenance of rehabilitated infrastructure.
Through national and provincial departments of agriculture, forestry and fisheries, measures will be taken to help farmers adopt climate-smart technologies to increase productivity and reduce emissions in agriculture.
