Habitat: In Cambodia, this species lives in Koh Kong, Pursat, Mondulkiri and Preah Vihear provinces and is also found throughout Indochina (including Cambodia, Vietnam, Laos, Thailand and Burma), Sumatra and Java.
Conservation status: Large squirrels live in low densities and are rarely found in the wild. This species is vulnerable to habitat loss and trapping or hunting.
Biology: The gray-bellied squirrel is a nocturnal animal and prefers to live in rainforests, grasslands, rubber plantations, oil palm, as well as mangrove forests.
* Habitat: Gray squirrels are found almost everywhere in Cambodia. It is also found in Malaysia, Sumatra, Java and Borneo.
Conservation status: Gray-skinned squirrels are relatively common in the rainforest and other plantations.
* Habitat: In Cambodia, colored squirrels are present in Koh Kong, Pursat, Mondulkiri, Kulen Promtep Wildlife Sanctuary and protected forest areas of Preah Vihear Province. It is also found in Burma, Thailand and southern Vietnam.
Conservation status: Colored squirrels are relatively densely populated and have a fast reproduction rate and are less endangered by the environment in which they live.
Habitat: In Cambodia, striped squirrels are present in the Keo Seima Biodiversity Conservation Area in Mondulkiri Province. This species is also found in eastern India, Bhutan, southern China, Burma, Thailand, Vietnam, Laos, Malaysia and so on.
Conservation status: Striped squirrels are relatively densely populated, have high reproduction rates, and are less endangered by the environment in which they live.
Habitat: Large-winged squirrel is present in almost all forest species in Cambodia, especially dense and semi-dense forests.
Conservation status: Large-winged squirrels have a low density, rarely fly or live in groups in the habitat where they live, and are endangered by habitat loss, trapping or hunting. Use meat as food and trade.
Biology: This species is found in dense forests up to 1000 m altitude. They nest in holes in the wood at least 4 mm above the ground. Its main food is fruit. 1-2 females at a time.
Conservation status: Small-winged squirrels are relatively densely populated, however, they are also under serious threat from habitat loss and hiding in remote areas of Cambodia's forests.
Conservation status: Small-winged squirrels live almost everywhere, are agile and have little risk of capture.
* Habitat: In Cambodia, Kangheng is present in Koh Kong, Pursat, Virachey National Park, Ratanakkiri and other areas. Kangen is also present in southern Burma, southern China, Laos and Thailand.
Conservation status: The lizard lives almost everywhere, is agile and has little risk of capture.
Beware of squirrels with sharp teeth that can cause injuries or sores, as some squirrels are infected by pests on their fruits and vegetables. On the other hand, some squirrel researchers can detect the presence of squirrels in an area through their fur.
