(Kampong Thom): Mr. Phong Vann, President of Kampong Thom Provincial Court, on June 21, 2024, gave an interview to Fresh News on the explanation of some procedures to adjust the civil status for people who are not cases. Complaint.
The President of the Provincial Court said that the Royal Government of the 7th legislature of the National Assembly as well as the Ministry of Justice has instructed the Capital-Provincial Courts to facilitate the procedure for people who have concerns related to the civil status case.
Mr. Phong Vann said that the solution to the civil status issue, which is not a lawsuit, is to make it easier for people who have relied on the court to ask for corrections to change the civil status data. Currently, the provincial court has cut the point that leads to delays, that is, as long as all the documents are issued to the people immediately, and there is no payment other than 5,000 riels.
"Slow or fast also depends on the people, whether they have submitted enough documents to the court or not," he said. When he requests to change his name or his parents' name or date of birth, a document must be attached to show his confidence in the court. "When he files a complaint, he will be examined the documents and asked for the purpose of revising the civil status and will be issued an order within two days."
The President of Kampong Thom Provincial Court also called on the Commune / Sangkat Administration before giving the civil registration rate to the people to focus on the necessary points:
* First: Have someone write a clear letter to the citizen requesting the civil status * Second: Must collect enough documents of parents before issuing a birth certificate to the child. In the case of a newborn, it is possible to find and refer to a hospital certificate. He said such precautions are to avoid causing inconvenience to the people.
Mr. Phong Vann further stated that these works are one of the attention of the Royal Government, including the reform of the judiciary, which Deputy Prime Minister Keut Rith, Minister of Justice has set three priorities: resolving external disputes. The judiciary expedites the settlement of cases and the promotion and decision-making. He added that the other three important points are the drug case, the traffic case and the civil status case.
