In a speech to business leaders, Anwar dismissed concerns about China's economic and military power, expressing a desire to work with China for mutual benefit. He also reiterated Malaysia's neutral stance and willingness to work with all countries, including China.
Li's visit, the first by a Chinese prime minister to Malaysia since 2015, highlighted the positive relations between China and Malaysia. The two leaders agreed that the South China Sea dispute should be resolved independently (without outside interference) and through dialogue.
China has been Malaysia's top trading partner since 2009, with bilateral trade reaching $ 98.8 billion last year. During the visit, several cooperation agreements were signed, which could bring in $ 2.8 billion in investment. Both countries have also agreed to extend visa-free travel.
The two countries also agreed to jointly request the inclusion of the Lion Dance, a traditional dance performed during Chinese New Year and other festivals, to the UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage List. A quarter of Malaysia's 33 million people are Chinese. Another 424 vehicles were inspected and fined on June 20, 2024
