According to Reuters, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang met with Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim in the administrative capital of Putrajaya after arriving in Kuala Lumpur on Tuesday.
"China is ready to work with Malaysia," Li said in a statement. He added that the focus areas include strategies for developing mutually beneficial cooperation and exchanges to promote China-Malaysia community building.
Following a closed-door meeting on Wednesday, Li and Anwar witnessed the signing of more than a dozen agreements on cooperation in areas ranging from the digital economy to green development, housing, tourism and tourism. Relationships, among other collaborations.
The new five-year cooperation agreement, which expires in 2028, provides strategic cooperation in areas such as trade and investment, Reuters reported, citing a statement issued after the meeting. Agriculture, production, infrastructure and financial services. The five-year program was first launched in 2013.
China also agreed to allow the import of fresh durian from Malaysia after Malaysia met the sanitation requirements, the statement added. Malaysia, one of the world's largest producers of durians, has previously been allowed to export only frozen durians and durian products to China, worth 1.19 billion ringgit ($ 253 million). ) In 2023.
China has been Malaysia's largest trading partner since 2009, and the Malaysian Ministry of Foreign Affairs said total trade would reach $ 98.9 billion by 2023. According to Reuters.
