ASEAN Secretary-General Receives Message from Two Ambassadors to ASEAN Meet with Secretary of State of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia and Other Programs
(Indonesia): On the morning of June 19, 2024, at the ASEAN Secretariat in Jakarta, Republic of Indonesia, Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Secretary-General of ASEAN, gave an opening speech at the ASEAN-China Future Relations Forum.
The forum is held under the theme "Towards a more comprehensive strategic partnership." The forum has three sessions, including (1) adherence to regionalism, (2) science, technology and artificial intelligence, and (3) dialogue with young people.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn also continued to meet with Dr. Un Kheang, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia.
On that occasion, the discussions and exchanges of views focused on the ongoing work of ASEAN and finding ways to bring ASEAN closer to the people of each member country, as well as finding ways to strengthen ASEAN cooperation for the better. Of the people of ASEAN.
The ASEAN Secretary-General emphasized that Malaysia's chairmanship of ASEAN next year is important as it is surrounded by key events such as the final revision of the ASEAN Community Vision 2025 and the adoption of the ASEAN Community Vision 2045 with And the four strategic plans of the ASEAN Community Pillar and ASEAN Connectivity.
In addition, the ASEAN Secretary-General commended Malaysia's leadership in co-chairing the High-Level Working Group Meeting on the ASEAN Post-2025 Vision.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn looks forward to a working visit to Malaysia this year to meet with key ministers of the three pillars of the ASEAN Community to support Malaysia's preparations for the 2025 ASEAN Chairmanship.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn also praised Malaysia's role as a facilitator for the ASEAN-Canada Dialogue Partnership from 2021 to 2024 and noted that from August 2024 to 2027, Malaysia will be the facilitator for ASEAN-China Dialogue Partnership
The ASEAN Secretary-General reaffirmed the ASEAN Secretariat's commitment to support and work closely with the Permanent Representative and Permanent Representative of Malaysia to the Permanent Mission to ASEAN and wished her success in her new role.
Sarah is Malaysia's sixth Permanent Representative to ASEAN since the Standing Committee was established in 2009. Sarah was Malaysia's Ambassador to Germany from 2017 to 2020 and Malaysia's Deputy Permanent Representative to ASEAN from 2010 to 2013.
Nicholas Panayiotou reaffirmed Cyprus' commitment to strengthening relations with ASEAN, both bilaterally and through the ASEAN-EU framework. Cyprus' commitment to building stronger ties with the region is evident with the recent establishment of an embassy in Jakarta.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn also encouraged the Ambassador to join the diplomatic community in Jakarta, especially with the Permanent Missions of ASEAN Member States to ASEAN, as well as ambassadors of other non-ASEAN countries to ASEAN.
Both sides noted the need to seek more cooperation through private sector participation, as well as increase trade, investment and tourism.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn stated that ASEAN looks forward to cooperating with Cyprus in areas of mutual benefit, with Cyprus having expertise and strength, both bilaterally and through the ASEAN-EU framework.
Panayiotou said it was an honor for Cyprus to present the credentials to the ASEAN Secretary-General. Cyprus is strongly committed to strengthening relations with ASEAN. I look forward to working with the ASEAN Secretariat and ASEAN members to learn from their experiences and visions.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Secretary-General of ASEAN, also continued to meet with young participants in the ASEAN-China Future Relations Forum.
The meeting aims to strengthen meaningful dialogue and interaction, hoping for a lively discussion between the ASEAN Secretary-General and youth participants on the important role of youth in shaping the future of cooperation and partnership. The region is part of the ASEAN-China Future Relations Forum.
On that occasion, Dr. Pham Lan Dung thanked the ASEAN Secretary-General for his support for the initiatives of the Vietnam Diplomatic Academy, including by participating in the ASEAN Future Forum held on April 23, 2024 in the country. Vietnam.
The two sides also exchanged views on possible cooperation between ASEAN and the Vietnam Diplomatic Academy.
Xi Jinping says Chinese army faces chronic problems in anti-corruption campaign
BEIJING: Chinese President Xi Jinping has said there are deep-rooted problems in China's military ideology, work style and discipline, state-run CCTV reported Wednesday. Continuing military purge against corruption.
"Cadres at all levels, especially senior officials, must come forward, dare to sacrifice their honor and show their shortcomings," he said. "They have to reflect deeply, make honest corrections and solve problems, starting from their thinking."
The ongoing anti-corruption crackdown on the People's Liberation Army (PLA) has been significant recently, with nine generals and a number of executives fired from the air defense industry.
In a keynote address to military cadres, Xi highlighted the complexity of the current challenges facing the People's Liberation Army (PLA) amid significant changes in national, party and military dynamics. He also promised to improve methods to detect and address various forms of corruption, including new and hidden types.
Xi's speech underscored his commitment to strengthening oversight of senior cadres and ensuring accountability in the military. The conference in Yan'an is an important time to address these issues, as it coincides with efforts to strengthen the Chinese People's Liberation Army and maintain discipline amid geopolitical tensions.
