Russian military launches 10-day naval exercises in Pacific and Sea of Japan
MOSCOW (Reuters) - A 10-day naval exercise began on Tuesday in the Pacific Ocean and in waters near Japan and South Korea, the Russian Defense Ministry said on Tuesday. Gems. This was reported by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, June 19, 2024.
Russia's Defense Ministry said the exercise was taking place in the Pacific Ocean, the Sea of Japan and the Sea of Okhotsk and involved a total of about 40 boats and ships, as well as about 20 aircraft and helicopters. The exercises include anti-submarine drills, fleet defense, joint missile launches against enemy fleets, and drone counter-attacks.
Moscow often holds regular military exercises in the Pacific to show off its naval muscles, but Western groups have been following the move with increasing attention since the war. The invasion of Ukraine erupted in 2022.
Moscow's latest military exercise will run until June 28, as tensions between Moscow and Japan and South Korea have risen as Russian President Vladimir Putin has spoken. Putin is in Pyongyang to meet with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un on a visit that is seen as strengthening ties between the two countries.
