(Phnom Penh): As of now, the official diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Vietnam are 57 years old. The two countries established official diplomatic relations with each other on June 24, 1967. The two villages share a common border of more than 1,270 km and the same Mekong River. Relations between the two countries are bound by historical, geographical and political ties.
Since the early 1990s, relations between Cambodia and Vietnam have entered a new political turning point, in which Vietnam maintained a socialist political regime, while Cambodia adopted a liberal, multi-party democracy and a constitutional monarchy. Re-created. Although the two countries have different political systems, relations between the two countries have not changed, but have been strengthened and expanded.
In the face of internal and external context, bilateral relations between the two countries are developing in many fields. Cambodia and Vietnam agree that strengthening and promoting friendly relations and cooperation between the two countries is of great importance to both nations. Overall, the bilateral foreign policy of Cambodia and Vietnam to each other underscores the strength of relations between the two countries.
Cambodia-Vietnam relations continue to diversify at both bilateral and multilateral levels and are strong in all areas of common interest. The two countries will work together to solve global problems, expand relations at all levels and in all forums, and strengthen mutual understanding, which will enable the two countries to achieve greater achievements at both the national and global levels.
With the above motto, we hope that the relationship between the two countries for the next century will continue to grow more broadly in all aspects, deeper, more effective and even better.
