Speaking at the 56th session of the UN Human Rights Council in Geneva on Tuesday, Türk highlighted the devastating effects of the conflict since October 7, 2023, noting that people More than 120,000 people, mostly women and children, have been killed or wounded in the escalating Israeli attacks.
He noted that nearly one million Palestinians have been forcibly displaced since Israel stepped up operations in Rafah in early May as the humanitarian situation deteriorated.
He also condemned the escalation of violence in the West Bank, including East Jerusalem, where Israeli security forces and Israeli settlers have killed 528 Palestinians, including 133 children since. October is coming. During the same period, 23 Israelis, including eight members of the Internal Security Force, were killed in clashes or attacks carried out by Palestinians.
The UN human rights chief also criticized the ongoing Israeli airstrikes on Gaza, noting the enormous human suffering and widespread devastation. He also expressed concern over the blocking of humanitarian aid and the arbitrary detention of thousands of Palestinians, calling for an end to these practices. He also called for the release of hostages by Palestinian militants.
Turk stressed the importance of respecting UN Security Council resolutions and the International Court of Justice, and called for an end to the occupation and the achievement of an international agreement on a two-state solution.
