A Reuters investigation found that the US military intended to raise doubts about the safety and effectiveness of vaccines and other life-saving supplies supplied by China. The U.S. military has been using fake Internet accounts to disguise itself as Filipinos to campaign against the Chinese vaccine.
A Chinese embassy spokesman said in a statement that "people around the world are outraged by the actions of the US military, which show hypocrisy, malice and double standards of the United States." "While respecting human rights, the United States is doing the opposite with respect to the fundamental human rights and lives of Filipinos," the Chinese embassy stressed.
The US Embassy in Manila has sent a request for comment to its Department of Defense. A senior US Defense Department official has acknowledged that the US military was involved in a covert campaign to destroy Chinese vaccines in developing countries, but declined to give further details.
A Pentagon spokesman was quoted as saying that the US military "uses various platforms, including social media, to combat attacks that have a devastating effect on the United States, its allies and allies." The report also states that China has launched a "campaign to spread false information to falsely accuse the United States of spreading Kovid-19 disease."
