China launches anti-dumping probe into European pork
BEIJING (Reuters) - China has launched an investigation into the sale of pork and pork offal imported from the European Union, a move likely to target Spain, the Netherlands and Denmark in response to tariffs on its electric vehicles. .
According to Reuters, an investigation announced by China's Ministry of Commerce on Monday will focus on pork for human consumption, including fresh, frozen and frozen meats, as well as pork intestines, bladder and stomach. The investigation will begin on June 17.
The Ministry of Commerce said the investigation was based on a complaint filed by the Chinese Livestock Association on June 6 on behalf of the local pork industry.
Following the European Commission's June 12 announcement that it would levy a 38.1 percent anti-subsidy tax on Chinese cars imported from July, world-class food companies are on high alert. For revenge tax from China.
Spain is the largest supplier of pork to China, and Spain's Interporc has said it will cooperate fully with the investigation by Chinese authorities.
European pork producers should be able to continue exporting to China duty-free while the investigation is pending a decision and tax declaration by the Chinese side. The Chinese Ministry of Commerce said the investigation should be completed by June 17, 2025, but could be extended for another six months if necessary.
