(Phnom Penh): Mr. Dith Tina, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that during the "Tree Day" ceremony on July 9, 2024, the Forestry Administration of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries plans to plant 9 luxury trees. 999 trees on an area of 30 hectares at the Banteay Angkor Forest Extension and Restoration Station, Takeo Province.
The remarks of the Minister of Agriculture were made on the morning of June 18, 2024, during the Minister's visit to the venue of the "Plant Day" celebration on July 9, which will be held at the Banteay Angkor Forest Extension and Rehabilitation Station in Khnar village. Lay Bo Commune, Tram Kak District, Takeo Province, under the highest presidency of His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Takeo province has hosted three national tree festivals on July 9, including: the first in 1993 at the Phnom Tamao Protected Zoo and Botanical Garden, and the second in 2008 at Banteay Angkor Forest Promotion and Restoration Station and the third time in 2014 at Phnom Tamao Protected Zoo and Botanical Garden.
The "Tree Day" festival was established in 1952 under the leadership of His Majesty the King, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, with the aim of inspiring the reforestation movement and for the people to participate in the preservation of forest property. Especially to cultivate the idea of conservation and love of the country's forests and natural environment.
Cambodia-China Friendship Radio CGTN Poll: Extreme Weather Frequent Interviewers Call on Developed Countries to Take Responsibility
Extreme weather events are occurring around the world, such as India, with rising temperatures, Spain suffering severe droughts, Germany suffering from floods and much of China being affected. From convective air, etc.
An online survey released by China Central Radio and Television (CGTN) found that 86.28 percent of respondents worldwide called on developed countries to take responsibility and respond responsibly. Active on global climate change.
When it comes to the causes of extreme weather, some experts have analyzed global climate change as a key factor. In this regard, 74.48% of respondents agreed that the frequent occurrence of extreme weather events reflects that global climate is rapidly deteriorating.
Eighty-five percent of respondents believe that in the face of the challenges of climate change, no country can escape. 82.69 percent of respondents pointed out that responding to climate change is not only a need for economic development, but also an international obligation of major countries to take responsibility.
