Cambodia-ASEAN Trade Union Confederation Condemns Central Organization
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian-ASEAN Trade Union Confederation (CACU) on the evening of June 17 issued a statement condemning the report of the Center for Labor Alliances and Human Rights (Central) accusing and inflating the issue of distortion of facts from Union status in Cambodia.
The statement came after Central released a 12-page report on June 4, entitled "Report on Barriers to Representation on Freedom of Association in Cambodia".
The statement states that Central's report relates only to the ILO BFC report and issues between unions and unions within a company. The content does not match the title of the report and can not be considered as an obstacle to the representation of all freedom of association in Cambodia, in other words, the report accuses and inflates the issue of distortion.
The Cambodian-ASEAN Trade Union Confederation states that the term union is an independent professional organization formed by a group of workers that they have agreed to set up for the purpose of protecting their rights and freedoms and serving their legitimate interests. . In Cambodia, any union is independent, only a few institutions have evaluated, leading to prejudice that this union is independent, that union is not independent.
The statement read: "Obviously, what we have always observed is that some unions have formed unions at the grassroots level. "First, when they responded, they accused the other union of not being independent, one union on this side."
However, the Cambodian-ASEAN Trade Union Confederation states that at present, the registration of any local union union can only be done in accordance with the conditions stipulated in the union law.
In the statement, the Cambodian-ASEAN Trade Union Confederation requested that Central review its report headline to avoid adversely affecting the feelings of existing investors and affecting the mood of new investors wishing to invest in Cambodia.
(Phnom Penh): The specialized force of the Department of Anti-Drug Crimes (A3) in cooperation with the Sihanoukville Provincial Commissioner cracked down on drug trafficking cases in 3 different destinations in Sihanoukville, Preah Sihanouk Province by arresting Chinese, Vietnamese and Nine Cambodians with more than 60 kilograms of drugs and more than 50 kilograms of chemical compounds.
The operation was carried out on the orders of General Sar Thet, National Police Commissioner, and Lt. Gen. Bun Pa Marilux, Deputy Commissioner General, in charge of the Anti-Drug Crime Department, and Lt. Gen. Prum Santhor, Deputy Commissioner General, in charge of the Anti-Drug Department. Lt. Gen. Kheng Sarath, Director of the Department of Anti-Drug Crimes.
Currently, the specialized force has prepared a case file and sent the suspects and exhibits to the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Court for processing.
Notably, in recent weeks, the Anti-Drug Department has cracked down on a number of major drug offenses, including transporting criminals in kilograms, and has arrested several perpetrators and sent them to court. . Interior Ministry Spokesman: 63 people arrested for criminal and drug offenses on June 17
