Bergen Stockholm: The summit, chaired by Ukrainian President Vladimir Putin in Switzerland and attended by more than 90 countries, failed to reach an agreement to end the war in Ukraine. This is according to the release by Reuters on June 16, 2024.
Despite widespread support for Ukraine among the participating countries, many challenges remain, especially over post-war solutions such as resettlement, troop withdrawal and security.
A draft statement calling Russia's actions a "war" and urging respect for Ukraine's territorial integrity may not have the support of all participating countries. Austrian Chancellor Karl Nehammer noted that some countries may refuse to sign the statement due to verbal issues, but they all agree that the war must end.
Russia and China did not attend the meeting. Russia dismissed the meeting as a waste of time and set out the opposite terms from a distance, while Ukraine insisted that Russia could not impose conditions on peace talks as long as the war continued.
Despite the differences, the meeting demonstrated the international community's commitment to finding a solution to end the war. But the meeting also highlighted the complexity of the conflict and the long road that remains to be achieved to achieve lasting peace in Ukraine.
