(Phnom Penh): Tourism experts say that if Cambodia attracts more Indian tourists, Cambodia can also attract investors from India to invest in tourism.
Ms. Chhay Sivlin, President of the Cambodian Tourism Association, said this while Cambodia and India began connecting direct flights on June 16, 2024 to attract tourism and mutual investment.
She said that in the past, Cambodia was also a popular tourist destination for Indians, although the two countries do not yet have direct flights. Their favorite destinations are Siem Reap and Phnom Penh, where they also take some pictures to introduce to others.
To attract more Indian tourists, she encourages the redevelopment of both tourist destinations, especially religious sites, which can host various festivals and the processing of locally made souvenirs.
"What is more attractive than what they advertise to each other is that services, investment in hospitality services, local processing areas, such as souvenirs of local products can also attract them," he said. Yes, too. ”
Attracting more Indian tourists will be able to attract Indian investors in the future. She notes that Indian investors are also those who have a lot of money and like to seek investment abroad.
"Before he invests, he has to come and study," she added. Usually, when there is a large number of people, it will make you think of the country's food, hospitality, and if the people invest directly, it will meet the demand and have a lot of support. "Therefore, this factor will attract investment from India as well."
She pointed out that the Cambodian Tourism Association has been promoting the potential of Siem Reap and Phnom Penh and other provinces as attractive areas, while the association and the Ministry of Tourism have been working with the Embassy of India for 2-3 years. And since the outbreak of Kovid 19 to work together to attract tourists to each other.
According to data from the Ministry of Tourism, in 2023, Cambodia received a total of more than 5.4 million tourists, including nearly 70,000 Indians. In the first quarter of 2024, Cambodia received a total of nearly 1.6 million tourists, including 20,000 Indian tourists.
