Reuters reports that after the draft proposal pending parliamentary approval, the special tariff on beer and strong liquor will be raised to 70 to 80 percent by 2026 and will gradually increase from 90 to 100 percent by 2030 compared to the current 65 percent.
"The price of alcoholic beverages and beer will increase by 20 percent in 2026 compared to 2025," the finance ministry said in a statement. Prices will continue to rise by 2 to 3 percent, depending on inflation, the ministry added.
"High tariffs are needed to help reduce the consumption of alcoholic beverages," the ministry added.
Vietnam's beer industry has already been affected by the country's strict alcohol driving laws, which have set zero alcohol levels for drivers since 2019.
Last year, revenue from the beer industry fell 11 percent and profits fell 23 percent, according to the Vietnam Alcohol and Beverage Association.
Shares in Sabeco, Vietnam's largest brewery, fell 3.96 percent on Friday morning after the ministry's request. The Ministry of Finance has also proposed an increase in excise taxes on non-alcoholic beverages and cigarettes. According to Reuters.
