(Phnom Penh): On June 15, 2024, this is the 62nd anniversary of the International Court of Justice in The Hague ruling that Cambodia won the case against the Thai side in the case of the Temple of Preah Vihear. Dr. Yong Pov, Secretary General of the Royal Academy of Cambodia and a political observer, took this opportunity to reveal the story of the Temple of Preah Vihear and Thailand's ambitions for the past.
It should be noted that Thaksin Shinawatra is a key figure in the current political crisis, which is popularly competing with the King of Thailand, as mentioned above.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, under the auspices of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, has been preparing documents since 2001 to include the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. However, due to a file error, it was too late because the old file was modified and rewritten. This document must be completed before 2006 and should be reviewed in January 2006, if not incorrect, one year later (2007) to be officially declared a World Heritage Site.
June 08, 2007: The Royal Government of Cambodia seeks support from friendly countries by holding a meeting at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs between Hor Namhong and Samdech Bol Panha Sok An with Ambassadors of friendly countries. Cambodia includes Canada, South Korea, Japan, India and Cuba.
June 20, 2007 - Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An, then Deputy Prime Minister and Minister in charge of the Office of the Council of Ministers, leads a Cambodian delegation from Phnom Penh to attend a UNESCO meeting in New Zealand.
July 17, 2007: US Ambassador to Cambodia Joseph Mussomeli says the US Embassy officially supports the listing of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site in a meeting with former Deputy Prime Minister Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An. Minister in charge of the Office of the Council of Ministers at the Office of the Council of Ministers.
July 24, 2007: A delegation from the United States Embassy in Canada, led by Mr. Var Kimhong, former Chairman of the Cambodian Boundary Commission, inspected the situation in Cambodia. Temple of Preah Vihear.
January 21, 2008: In Phnom Penh, a meeting of the High Committee for Monitoring the application for registration of the Temple of Preah Vihear as an official sanctuary in the UNESCO World Heritage List was held.
January 26, 2008: The Office of the Council of Ministers of Cambodia releases a press release related to the January 21, 2008 meeting.
March 3, 2008: Incumbent Thai Prime Minister Samak Sundaravet reaffirms that the Thai government has not opposed Cambodia's inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. Of the Thai government announced in front of the Cambodian leadership.
March 3, 2008: Teruo Jinnai, UNESCO Representative in Cambodia, speaks in Banteay Meanchey province during the Cambodian National Culture Day: "Cambodia is currently in the process of preparing to include the magnificent Temple of Preah Vihear on the World Heritage List. "UNESCO has been working closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia on this registration."
May 06, 2008: Cambodia and Thailand issue a joint statement after a meeting on the Temple of Preah Vihear between the Cambodian delegation led by Samdech Sok An, Deputy Prime Minister in charge of the Office of the Council of Ministers and the Thai delegation led by Mr. Virasadee Futrakol, Secretary General of the Thai Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
June 13, 2008: Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia, requests the representatives of the Royal Government to maintain good relations with the Royal Government of Thailand in order to promote the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site. He was born in early July 2008 in Quebec City, Canada.
In the spirit of goodwill and coordination, the Kingdom of Cambodia accepts the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear in the World Heritage List, with no buffer zone on the north and west sides of the temple at this time.
