Reuters reports, citing a press release issued by the IMF, that the implementation of Sri Lanka's crisis-hit reforms under the bailout program is strong and signs of economic recovery are emerging. Up. But the lender said the economy "remains vulnerable and the path to debt sustainability remains unstable."
Sri Lanka, hit by its financial crisis, has plunged into its worst financial crisis in more than seven decades after its foreign exchange reserves fell to a record low in 2022. After failing to pay its debts two years ago, the country has been struggling to recover from the crisis. The South Asian economy contracted 2.3 percent last year.
As part of a $ 2.9 billion bailout program with the IMF, Sri Lanka has agreed to reform its state-owned companies to make them profitable.
According to Reuters, Sri Lanka's Foreign Minister Ali Sabry said the IMF approval was "a testament to our commitment to pushing forward economic reforms and securing a future." "Glorious for all Sri Lankans."
World Bank cuts Myanmar's economic growth forecast (Video inside) (WASHINGTON) - The World Bank (WB) said on Wednesday that Myanmar's economy, torn by armed conflict, will be around 1 percent for the year. Fiscal 2024-2025 Due to the increase in violence, labor shortage and the depreciation of the currency makes it more difficult to do business. According to Reuters.
In December, the World Bank forecast that Myanmar's economy would grow by about 2 percent over the same period after estimating 1 percent GDP growth in the fiscal year ending March. 2024.
Reuters quoted a report released by the World Bank as saying: "The drop in growth forecasts for fiscal year 2024/2025 is largely due to persistent high inflation and labor shortages. "Foreign exchange and power shortages, all of which are likely to have a greater impact on activity than previously expected."
The Southeast Asian nation of about 55 million people has been in political and economic turmoil since the 2021 coup, when the military overthrew an elected civilian government. Faced with a broader armed struggle against its rule, Myanmar's military regime earlier this year announced a military plan to replenish its lost troops.
The World Bank said: "The declaration of military service in February 2024 has led to an increase in migration to rural areas and abroad, leading to an increase in reports of labor shortages in the country. In some industries.
The military regime also lost access to some of the mainland borders with China and Thailand, leading to a sharp decline in land trade.
Excluding natural gas, cross-border exports fell 44 percent, the World Bank said. "Land border imports fell by half, accounting for 71 percent of total imports." Overall, exports fell 13 percent and imports fell 20 percent in the six months to March 2024 compared to the same period last year, the World Bank said. According to Reuters.
