The Republic of Ireland meets with the team of HelloTrust Cambodia in Siem Reap to discuss the effectiveness of support for humanitarian mine action
In the three decades following the start of demining efforts, Cambodia continues to face significant threats from landmines and other explosive remnants of war. Since 1979, more than 65,000 Cambodian men, women and children have been killed or seriously injured by landmines and unexploded ordnance.
The death toll and injuries have dropped sharply (from 2,069 in 1993 to under 30 in 2023) due to the dedicated mine action efforts of all national and international operators.
HelloTrust, an internationally recognized organization with decades of experience in humanitarian demining, will use the Irish assistance to deploy seven demining teams, one EOD research team and one demining team. Mine and Mine Education (EORE) 1 group.
"Since 1994, Irish Aid has supported the HelloTrust humanitarian demining program in Cambodia," she said in handing over the equipment, which was sponsored by the Irish Embassy.
"We are extremely proud that the Irish Aid Task Force cleared 31,343 mines in 361 villages and returned more than 3,684 hectares of safe land to communities for use in agriculture and development," she said. Sustainable. "This assistance has a real positive effect on the community today and also benefits the next generation."
"Disarmament is fundamental to Ireland's foreign policy," said Niall Tierney, director of the's Global Program for Humanitarian Aid. The Republic of Ireland continues to play a leading role in efforts to promote disarmament and non-proliferation. Our work with HelloTrust Cambodia is part of this global commitment. ”
