Bank of Thailand maintains benchmark rate at 2.5%
BANGKOK: The Bank of Thailand kept its benchmark interest rate unchanged for four consecutive meetings on Wednesday, as expected, despite repeated calls from the Thai government to lower it. Interest rates to help revive Southeast Asia's second-largest economy
The Bank of Thailand's Monetary Policy Committee (BOT) voted 6-1 to keep the benchmark interest rate unchanged at 2.5 percent, the highest level in more than a decade, Reuters reported.
The decision comes as three of the 27 economists in a Reuters poll expected the central bank to keep interest rates unchanged this week. The other three economists forecast a 0.25 percent rate cut.
The Thai government has repeatedly called for lower interest rates to revive the economy, which has grown slower than its regional neighbors.
The world faces crude oil surplus by 2030 as renewable energy increases
The Paris-based energy agency said this would result in a surplus of eight million barrels per hour, which the oil market should be prepared for.
"As the recovery from the epidemic loses momentum, the shift to clean energy has accelerated and China's economic structure has shifted to growing global oil demand," said IEA Energy Executive Director Fatih Birol. "It is declining and will reach peak demand by 2030."
"With the huge supply surplus emerging this decade, oil companies may want to make sure their strategies and business plans are ready for this change," Birol said.
