EU to impose tariffs of up to 38% on Chinese electric cars
The European Commission announced on Wednesday that it would impose an additional 38.1 percent tariff on Chinese electric cars imported from next month, a move likely to retaliate. From China. According to Reuters.
According to Reuters, China's Ministry of Commerce said it would closely monitor the development and take all necessary measures to protect the legitimate rights of Chinese companies.
The EU temporary tariff is set to take effect on July 4, and an investigation into the subsidy is set to continue until November 2, when the permanent tariff, which normally lasts five years, could increase. Applies if subsidies are found.
The European Commission has said it will apply a 21 percent tariff for companies deemed to have cooperated with the investigation and 38.1 percent for non-cooperating companies. The new tariff will add 10 percent to the EU's existing tariff. Western manufacturers such as Tesla and BMW, which export cars from China to Europe, are considered co-operatives in the investigation.
Senior official of the Ministry of Commerce: Promoting enterprises and informal economic development to contribute to economic growth and job creation
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chheang Christoff, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce, said that the promotion of enterprises, entrepreneurship, informal economic development and the creation of new business opportunities, crisis-resistant and innovative to contribute to economic growth and job creation.
Mr. Chheang Christoff said this on June 12, 2024, on the occasion of Ms. Cham Nimmol, Minister of Commerce, presiding over the seminar on "Dissemination of Import Demand of the People's Republic of China, the Republic of Singapore and Republic of Turkey ”at the Ministry of Commerce, with the participation of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, the Cambodian Association of Small and Medium Enterprises, enterprises and many other Cambodian exporters.
The purpose of this workshop is to provide opportunities for Cambodian traders and entrepreneurs to gain new knowledge and export procedures to ensure the effective export of domestic products to international markets, especially to understand the market needs, business environment. Consumer ideas and tastes in the People's Republic of China, the Republic of Singapore and the Republic of Turkey.
Mr. Chheang Christoff added that under the leadership of His Excellency Hun Manet, the Prime Minister has set out the "Pentagon Strategy - Phase 1" as the third priority on further strengthening and expanding domestic and global markets for small and medium enterprises and New business through the implementation of state support mechanisms and public procurement, as well as providing support and encouragement to the establishment of business networks.
Mr. Chheang Christoff also stated that this important event provides an opportunity for Cambodian entrepreneurs, traders, businessmen and businessmen to learn more about foreign direct demand for Cambodian products, import and export procedures, product design, prices and competition. Incorporate new technologies in the improvement of its products according to orders and other relevant information.
He also claimed that the participation of speakers from the three countries and speakers in Cambodia now is an important input in the development of trade and search for overseas markets. Immediately after leaving Spain for Germany, Deputy Prime Minister Sun Chanthol met with UVEX Business Director.
After presenting to the Cambodian delegation and especially hearing about the potential and support of the Royal Government of Cambodia, UVEX will consider selecting Cambodia to further expand its investment.
