Temperatures are expected to rise to 43 degrees Celsius on Wednesday and Thursday this week in parts of the Mediterranean, driven by south winds, bringing in hot air and dust from North Africa.
Greece is one of the worst affected countries in Europe. Last year, rising temperatures triggered wildfires and intermittent rains, causing the worst flooding ever, both of which destroyed crops and livelihoods.
Scientists say last winter was the hottest ever and that little rain created the conditions for more wildfires.
Similar weather conditions have occurred across southern Europe, including Portugal, France, Spain and Italy, where fires have killed dozens of people. The Asian Development Bank (ADB) has announced that it will continue to strengthen cooperation with the Royal Government to improve the lives of Cambodians.
(Phnom Penh): The Asian Development Bank (ADB), one of Cambodia's key development partners, has announced it will continue to strengthen cooperation with the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Prime Minister Hun Manet, to improve the lives of Cambodians.
During the meeting, the President of the ADB expressed his deep gratitude to Samdech Hun Sen for taking the time to allow him and his colleagues to pay a courtesy call and discuss work. He also congratulated the President, who was elected by the National Assembly as the Prime Minister of Cambodia for the seventh term of the National Assembly in August 2023.
The President of the ADB also expressed confidence that under the wise leadership of the President of Cambodia, there will be further strong development.
In response, President Hun Manet welcomed the President of the ADB Bank for his visit to Cambodia and expressed his pleasure and congratulations on the official opening of the ADB Representative Office in Cambodia.
The Prime Minister also thanked the ADB for its commitment to continue to support the Royal Government of Cambodia, noting that the Royal Government of Cambodia, the seventh legislature of the National Assembly, has launched new initiatives to ensure growth. Sustainable and resilient economy in order to improve the living standards of the people and achieve the vision of Cambodia 2050 to become a high-income country.
(Phnom Penh): On the afternoon of June 12, 2024, the Ministry of Health issued a press release on the prevention of the spread of influenza and Kovid 19. Dangkor District Prepares for Health Sector Reform Under $ 11 Million Support
(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Municipal Administration and the Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) in Cambodia have announced the launch of a project to improve health services in Dangkor district, a fast-growing area, and this is great news for Khan residents. Worms.
Dangkor district is a growing industrial area and is located near the new international airport in Takhmao city. These factors have led to an increase in the population, so the demand for quality public services is also increasing, especially health services.
Recognizing this important need, the Phnom Penh Municipal Administration requested the support of the KOICA Cambodia Office to improve Dangkor Referral Hospital last year and also agreed to provide assistance to implement this project.
The project has a total budget of $ 11 million set aside for the construction of a new hospital building and state-of-the-art medical equipment. This significant assistance will help develop Dangkor Referral Hospital to be a leader in the field of health, improve the quality of services and strengthen the emergency department on fractures and training programs to strengthen the capacity of hospital staff to ensure that they can provide health services. Best to the community.
Dangkor Referral Hospital will be Phnom Penh's first CPA2 level hospital under a partnership between the Cambodian and Korean governments through this project. This is a transition to change the level of quality of health services that people in Dangkor will receive more advanced medical services through the achievement of the project and contribute to alleviate the burden of congestion in the hospital. National.
The Dangkor Referral Hospital project will serve as a model for other health facilities in Phnom Penh in terms of service quality, referral system and management plan in line with the Royal Government's first phase of the Pentagon Strategy and the policy of the Ministry of Health.
It should also be noted that KOICA is a bilateral aid agency of the Government of the Republic of Korea. Since opening its office in Phnom Penh in 2003, KOICA Cambodia has undergone various development cooperation in the fields of education, health and rural development.
These efforts have developed pediatric therapies in the eyes, ears, nose, throat, in addition to the scope of general therapy or existing surgery.
Community outreach programs are implemented in needy areas along with raising public awareness. In addition, KOICA implements a project to prevent and treat non-communicable diseases in Prek Pnov district in Phnom Penh and Thmor Kol district, Battambang province.
These projects have strengthened local awareness, improved non-communicable disease prevention activities, and strengthened the capacity of referral hospitals and health centers to act as pioneers for the Cambodian government in strengthening non-communicable disease management. Infected.
In connection with these efforts, the new project, which focuses on improving public health services at Dangkor Referral Hospital, is KOICA's new obligation to increase the scope of its cooperation. In addition to supporting national hospitals and other district and provincial hospitals, the project aims to support the development of CPA1 Hospital into the first CPA2 Hospital in Phnom Penh.
By supporting the Referral Hospital on the outskirts of Phnom Penh, this project will act as a catalyst for the development of the health sector in Phnom Penh and also pave the way for further cooperation between KOICA and Phnom Penh Capital Hall to improve the quality of life of the people. Cambodia is getting better.
