Deputy Prime Minister Sun Chanthol's article in response to the broadcast of the French news agency AFP. On May 30, 2024, AFP published an article on the Funan Techo canal, entitled "Cambodia will start construction of the controversial Prek Chik project in August."
The Deputy Prime Minister further commented in the article that the Funan Techo canal project is an ambitious initiative aimed at enhancing Cambodia's transport capacity and economic independence. The project is designed to serve the general population and has no purpose or plan for the use of naval vessels.
The Deputy Prime Minister emphasized that according to Article 53 of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia, the establishment of foreign military bases in the territory of Cambodia is strictly prohibited, which can ensure that the canal will not be used for service. No military purpose.
A detailed environmental impact assessment with the participation of 48 international experts ensures that the canal project will not have an impact on the Mekong River.
Irrigation models predict that the impact on the Mekong River runoff is only 0.06 percent in the dry season and no impact in the rainy season.
The project will create new habitats and prevent the intrusion of salt water, which provides protection for biodiversity in the Mekong Delta.
With a direct waterway connection between Phnom Penh and the sea, Kep Prek Chik will enhance Cambodia's trade efficiency, create jobs, promote industrial development, promote tourism, improve irrigation and attract investment. Direct from abroad. These benefits will transform Cambodia's economic landscape into a major logistics hub in the region.
