Cambodian Restaurant Association presents the results of a survey in the restaurant sector to seek government intervention
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Restaurant Association on June 11, 2024 presented a survey to find out the problems that the restaurant industry is facing and find solutions to support the restaurant sector.
The survey aims to provide specific data that reveals the realities of the restaurant sector and as a basis for advocating with the government for the necessary support, highlighting key interventions that are highly effective.
Two and a half years after the expiration of measures to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 and in times of economic uncertainty, the restaurant industry in Cambodia is facing severe stress.
Detailed Survey The Cambodian Restaurant Association surveyed a total of 118 companies representing more than 400 restaurant operators of different types of restaurants, expressing their views on the current state of the sector.
* Survey shows diversity of restaurant types:
1: Luxury and regular restaurants: Both types of restaurants represent 33.1%. 2: Fast food restaurants: represent 25.4%. Other types of restaurants: Including 14.4%, which shows the diversity and creativity in the restaurant sector.
Based on its geographical location, Phnom Penh is the main market with 82.2% of restaurants located in Phnom Penh. Other key locations include Siem Reap with 28% of restaurants, Sihanoukville with 25.4% and 17.8% of restaurants based in Battambang.
According to a survey by the Restaurant Association, the challenges of operating a large portion of the business, about 72.9% of the restaurants participating in the survey, have been in business for more than five years, indicating that the business stands on its own two feet. The market will be strong, but is facing unprecedented challenges.
The cost of renting a business location has a high cost of up to 66.1% and the cost of goods 64.4% is the cost that affects the business the most.
These factors led to a sharp drop in profit margins for businesses of 41.5%, reflecting financial tensions in the sector.
Arnaud Darc, President of the Cambodian Restaurant Association, said: “The restaurant sector is in a worrying state, despite the resilient business and adaptation efforts, the current economic pressure is not certain without supportive measures. From the government.
He said the survey highlighted the urgent need for tax relief measures and financial assistance to ensure the sustainability and full recovery of the restaurant sector. Fifteen high school students in Kratie province escaped from school and were taken to a café to be educated
