ASEAN Secretary-General pays working visit to US
(US): At the invitation of the United States Department of State, ASEAN Secretary-General Dr. Kao Kim Hourn led a delegation from the ASEAN Secretariat to pay a working visit to the United States and meet with leaders and stakeholders in Washington, DC. United States.
On the morning of June 10, 2024, in Washington DC, USA, the Secretary-General of ASEAN met with Sara Minakara, US Special Adviser on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, to discuss the strengthening of the rights of persons with disabilities throughout ASEAN. During the meeting, the talks focused on strengthening ASEAN-US cooperation on promoting equal opportunities and increasing access to rights for people with disabilities.
ASEAN Secretary-General Dr. Kao Kim Hourn meets with US Deputy Secretary of State Kurt Campbell. During the meeting, the two sides reviewed the dynamic progress of the ASEAN-US comprehensive strategic partnership and discussed regional and international issues of mutual interest and concern. Both sides also stressed their support for Laos, which holds the ASEAN chairmanship this year.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn met with Anne Neuberger, Deputy Chief of National Security Adviser to the United States. On that occasion, the two sides exchanged views on expanding ASEAN-US cooperation on cyber security and emerging technologies.
Later in the day, he met with the ASEAN Committee in Washington, DC. On that occasion, the Secretary-General of ASEAN updated the Committee on Recent Developments in ASEAN and ASEAN-US Relations. He also thanked the committee for its important role in promoting the ASEAN-US partnership.
The Washington Post made the report based on information it received from State Department officials. U.S. officials, speaking on condition of anonymity, told the newspaper that the State Department had decided to lift a more than a decade-long ban on the use of US weapons by the Azov Brigade after a new analysis found no evidence. No human rights abuses by this Ukrainian brigade.
Leahy Law is a U.S. law aimed at banning the provision of US military aid to foreign military entities that have been found to be in violation of human rights. The Azov Brigade is an ultra-right-wing and ultra-nationalist special force under the control of the Ukrainian National Guard, which has been involved in the fight against Russian-backed separatist forces in eastern Ukraine since 2014.
