IMF lowers Philippine growth forecast for 2024
The Philippine economy is likely to grow 6 percent this year, less than forecast in April, but the pace of growth will be faster than last year, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) said on Monday. Due to domestic demand rebounding. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports that the IMF's latest estimate fell from its April forecast of 6.2 percent, but was higher than the 5.5 percent increase in 2023. This figure is the lowest level of the Philippine government's target of 6 to 7 percent for 2024.
However, the IMF maintained its 2025 growth forecast for the Southeast Asian nation at 6.2 percent.
"Growth is expected to recover ... due to stronger consumption, higher public and private investment and recovery," the IMF said in a statement. "Return of exports."
