(Phnom Penh): Tuol Kork District Administration would like to inform the public that in the past, there were a small number of people in Village 3, Sangkat Boeung Kak I, whose houses were built on the public road on State Road 347 (from Street 598 to Street 528) made a letter requesting the intervention of the Electricity Authority of Cambodia to move the electricity pole from the back of their homes to the front.
In this case, the Tuol Kork district administration would like to clarify that Leak 347 is 270 meters long and 30 meters wide from the beginning. But later, when the state has not yet developed this road, some people came to take the public road land as housing. The electric poles built on this road are on the public sidewalk and are built in accordance with the standards and technical plans of Electricite du Cambodge.
Due to heavy congestion at the Camko City traffic light, the Phnom Penh Municipal Administration has built a section of Road 347 with a width of 20 meters to facilitate and avoid traffic congestion in the above areas, the state has already addressed the impact with some residents. To build this road temporarily. In particular, the electricity poles built are on the public sidewalks of the state, not on the land owned by the people.
In the future, if there is a need to widen this road, the state will continue to address the impact on the people who build on the state's public roads. As mentioned above, all citizens and the public, please be informed.
Malaysia launches diesel subsidy reform, prices to rise by about 50%
The Malaysian government said last month that its plans to reduce diesel subsidies this year are expected to save about 4 billion ringgit ($ 853.24 million) a year. The savings are expected to be used to help low-income people.
The ministry will start setting diesel prices to match market prices, CNA reported, citing a press release issued by the Malaysian Ministry of Finance on Sunday.
The ministry said retail prices of diesel would rise to 3.35 ringgit per liter starting at midnight on Monday at all petrol stations across the Malaysian peninsula. This is the non-subsidized market price based on the average price in May 2024 according to the automatic pricing mechanism formula.
The price of diesel will remain the same at 2.15 ringgit per liter in the Malaysian states and territories of Borneo and for subsidized trucks under the fuel management system. Government subsidized diesel.
The Ministry of Finance added that the low diesel price is also set for fishermen and public transport by road, such as buses and ambulances.
Diesel is sold in four different prices in Malaysia, according to subsidies: 1.65 ringgit for fishermen, 1.88 ringgit for public transport, 2.15 ringgit for commercial vehicles and Private as well as non-subsidized market value for the commercial sector, which rose to 3.6 ringgit earlier this year. According to the CNA.
