(Phnom Penh): With the approval of His Excellency Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia and with the coordination of the Embassy of the French Republic in Cambodia, Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Deputy Prime Minister in charge of the Office of the Council of Ministers and Chairman The Legal Advisory Council has invited a delegation to visit Paris, France from 9 to 15 June 2024.
This is the Deputy Foreign Minister's second visit abroad after his recent visit to Australia in May 2024.
1: Exchange experiences and knowledge related to strengthening the functions and duties of the Office of the Council of Ministers as an important staff of the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers with quality and efficiency, especially related to the day-to-day coordination functions of the Council of Ministers.
2. Exchanging experiences related to legal reform and principles of drafting and reviewing draft laws and other legal documents and resolving special administrative disputes related to overlapping jurisdictions.
3: Exchange experiences on working with the National Assembly-Senate.
The purpose of this visit is to contribute to further strengthening and expanding the friendship, solidarity, fraternity and fruitful cooperation between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the French Republic, especially on the basis of the special and close relationship between Samdech Techo Hun Sen. Former Prime Minister and President of the Senate and President of France Emmanuel Macron.
It should also be noted that Cambodia and France have similar administrative and legal systems due to the influence of the French protectorate in Cambodia from 1863 to 1953.
In this sense, this visit is very important for the Cambodian delegation to gain more lessons, experiences and good practices that can be used as a basis for improving the working system and reforming the work system to strengthen the capacity and capacity of the institution. In particular, to strengthen the quality and efficiency in the implementation of some key and core functions of the Office of the Council of Ministers, such as:
