Cambodian union leaders: Central report does not reflect the realities of unions in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Trade union leaders in Cambodia consider Central's report to be unacceptably biased and misleading, which does not reflect the realities of trade unions in Cambodia. The remarks by Cambodian trade union leaders come after Central released a report on trade union freedom in Cambodia on June 4, 2024.
Mr. Sam Soeun, President of the National Labor Union of Cambodia (NLC), pointed out that the report presented by Central on Freedom of Association in Cambodia did not reflect the realities of the union situation in Cambodia.
"It does not reflect the situation in Cambodia, the labor sector in Cambodia is better, the freedoms of the unions are better, and our workers get better working conditions and decent money," Sam Soeun said. "Wages and other conditions."
He added that Central should conduct more detailed and detailed research before issuing a report, not just minority opinions and conclusions.
Meanwhile, Mr. Un Dara, President of the Cambodian Union of Light Workers' Union, emphasized that the statement of Central Organization is not realistic and acceptable to all parties.
At the same time, unions in Cambodia criticized Central's report as unacceptable representation, as only 10 people were interviewed and the situation of unions in Cambodia as a whole was assessed.
It should be noted that currently the number of unions in Cambodia has increased from more than 3,000 in 2016 to more than 6,000 in 2024. As for the doubling of trade unions in Cambodia in the short term, Cambodia is seen as a paradise of non-discriminatory freedom of association.
Report does not reflect the truth Mr. Un Dara, President of the Cambodian Trade Union Confederation of Light Workers
