Former Cambodian refugee camp in Trat province is the first destination of Cambodian, Thai and Vietnamese delegations
(Trat): After arriving at Trat Multi-Purpose Port on June 5, a Cambodian, Thai and Vietnamese delegation preparing to participate in the 2024 Trat Business Fair visited the former Khmer refugee camp in Khlong Yai District, Trat Province. Which is now called "Sala Reachkarun".
Sala Reach Karun was originally a Cambodian refugee camp that escaped massacre during Democratic Kampuchea. The camp was established in 1978 under the patronage of Her Majesty the Queen of Thailand, President of the Thai Red Cross, at a time when he had compassion for the Cambodian people affected by the war and the Khmer Rouge.
Until 1986, the refugee camps were disbanded, with some people being transferred to other camps in Thailand under the auspices of the United Nations, some to third countries, and some to other countries. Repatriated. Since then, the camp has been closed, but today it has become a museum in Khlong Yai district, Trat province, "Sala Reach Karun" and a tourist destination in the province.
After visiting the Royal Palace, the three delegations continued to visit the Chinese-style Temple of the Immaculate Conception in Khlong Yai District, Trat Province, a religious tourist destination in the province. .
